[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 377,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 176,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57357,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 176,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumour is a fibroblastic/myofibroblastic neoplasm of intermediate biological potential, usually with a prominent inflammatory infiltrate of chiefly lymphocytes and plasma cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 378,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 177,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57358,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 177,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8825/1 Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumour</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 379,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 178,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57359,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 178,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F30.Y &amp; XH66Z0 Other specified benign neoplasm of breast &amp; Myofibroblastic tumour NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 380,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 179,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57360,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 179,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> inflammatory pseudotumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 381,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 180,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57361,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 180,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epithelioid inflammatory myofibroblastic sarcoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 382,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 181,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57362,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 181,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The breast is rarely involved, with &lt;&nbsp;25&nbsp;reported cases {15944952; 12968967; 23255901; 25772857}. Most cases occur in the respiratory tract, abdominal cavity, and retroperitoneum {17938159}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 383,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 182,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57363,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 182,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumour of the breast presents as a painless, circumscribed mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 384,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 183,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57364,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 183,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours of the breast most often affect young to middle-aged females, although the age range is broad {7611533; 11979371}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 385,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 184,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57365,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 184,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 386,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 185,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57366,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 185,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>About two&nbsp;thirds of inflammatory myofibroblastic tumours harbour receptor tyrosine kinase gene rearrangements, most often involving the <em>ALK</em> locus at 2p23, with diverse fusion partners {24091028}. Approximately 5% of inflammatory myofibroblastic tumours harbour <em>ROS1</em> gene fusions; other rare gene fusions involve <em>NTRK3</em>, <em>PDGFRB</em>, and <em>RET</em> {24875859; 25723109; 26647767; 27259007; 28731868}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 393,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 206,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57367,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 206,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most tumours are &lt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;cm in size, with white to grey and sometimes yellow cut surfaces.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 387,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 186,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57368,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 186,
        "headingText": "<p>Loose fascicles of uniform, plump spindle cells with vesicular chromatin, small nucleoli, and pale eosinophilic to amphophilic cytoplasm are typically observed {7611533}. The stroma may be myxoid or collagenous, usually containing an inflammatory infiltrate dominated by lymphocytes and plasma cells, with fewer eosinophils and neutrophils. Some tumours exhibit a compact, fascicular architecture with minimal stroma. A subset of tumour cells may resemble ganglion cells. Mitotic activity is low and necrosis is usually absent.</p>\r\n<p>Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumours are nearly always positive for SMA, and more than half of cases are also positive for desmin. Keratin expression is observed in 20&ndash;30% of cases. CD34, S100, SOX10, and EMA are negative. About 60% of inflammatory myofibroblastic tumours express ALK (usually with a diffuse cytoplasmic pattern) {11684952; 28731868} and approximately 5% of cases express ROS1, correlating with underlying <em>ALK</em> and <em>ROS1</em> gene fusions, respectively {25612511; 25723109}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-27T11:41:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 388,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 187,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57369,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 187,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 389,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 188,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57370,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 188,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Confirmation of an appropriate fusion gene supports the diagnosis. However, molecular confirmation is not required if ALK immunohistochemistry is definitively positive.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 392,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 191,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57371,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 191,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> loose fascicles of plump spindle cells without substantial atypia or pleomorphism; inflammatory infiltrate of lymphocytes and plasma cells; consistent SMA expression; frequent ALK expression.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 390,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 189,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57372,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 189,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 391,
    "bookid": 32,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 190,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57373,
        "bookId": 32,
        "chapterId": 70,
        "headingId": 190,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumours of the breast have a low rate of local recurrence (~15%) {24741129}. Distant metastasis is rare (occurring in &lt;&nbsp;5% of cases) {23255901}; histological features do not reliably predict clinical behaviour {17414097}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-09-03T14:29:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]